var WORKSHOP = {
  "title": "Zephyr Device Driver: Adding LPS22HB Sensor Driver to an nRF54LM20 Project",
  "chapters": [
    {
      "title": "Welcome & Introduction",
      "steps": [
        {
          "title": "How to use this guide",
          "description": "<div>The Workshop Guide can be opened in any standard web browser or, if preferred, directly in VS Code using its integrated browser. This allows you to follow the hands-on, step-by-step instructions without leaving your development environment.</div><div><br></div><div>The following list outlines what is possible with this workshop guide:</div><ul dir=\"ltr\"><li>This guide walks you through the hands-on step by step.</li>\n<li>Use <strong>Next step</strong> to move forward, and <strong>Previous</strong> to go back.</li>\n<li>Chapters must be completed in order — a locked chapter (🔒) opens once every step in the chapters before it is checked off.</li>\n<li>Click a chapter's title in the sidebar to see a short summary of that chapter, if one is available.</li>\n<li>Your progress (steps completed / remaining) is shown at the top and updates automatically.</li>\n<li>Use the <strong>Sidebar</strong> switch (top row) to collapse the chapter list, and <strong>Hide images</strong> to hide pictures in the steps.</li>\n<li>Some steps have <strong>ⓘ More info</strong> buttons under their description — click one for extra background or troubleshooting tips.</li>\n<li>Click <strong>Copy</strong> on a code snippet to copy it to your clipboard.</li>\n<li>Open the ⚙ <strong>Settings</strong> menu (top right) to switch between dark and light mode, or to clear your saved progress.</li></ul>",
          "codeBlocks": [],
          "images": [],
          "moreInfo": [],
          "codeBlocksNoCopy": []
        },
        {
          "title": "Enable Auto Save in VS Code",
          "description": "<p dir=\"ltr\">Throughout this workshop you'll be editing several files (source code, DeviceTree overlays, configuration files) and then triggering builds or flashing. To avoid confusion later — for example, forgetting to save before a build and wondering why your changes don't take effect — we recommend enabling <strong>Auto Save</strong> now, once, for the rest of the workshop.</p>\n<p dir=\"ltr\"><strong>Steps:</strong></p>\n<ol dir=\"ltr\">\n<li>Open the <strong>File</strong> menu in the VS Code menu bar.</li>\n<li>Click <strong>Auto Save</strong> to toggle it on. A checkmark next to the entry confirms it's enabled.</li></ol><div>That's it — from now on, any change you make to a file is written to disk automatically after a short delay (or when you switch focus away from the editor), so you won't need to press <strong>Ctrl+S</strong> / <strong>Cmd+S</strong> throughout the workshop.</div>",
          "codeBlocks": [],
          "images": [],
          "moreInfo": [
            {
              "label": "But I want to save it manually",
              "content": "If you prefer to save manually and know what you're doing, you can skip this step — just remember to save (<strong>Ctrl+S</strong> / <strong>Cmd+S</strong>) before every build in the steps ahead, since an unsaved change will not be picked up by the build system."
            }
          ],
          "codeBlocksNoCopy": []
        },
        {
          "title": "Overview of Our Project",
          "description": "<span style=\"color: rgb(31, 35, 40); font-family: &quot;Mona Sans VF&quot;, -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, &quot;Segoe UI&quot;, &quot;Noto Sans Backtick Fix&quot;, &quot;Noto Sans&quot;, Helvetica, Arial, sans-serif, &quot;Apple Color Emoji&quot;, &quot;Segoe UI Emoji&quot;; font-size: 16px;\">The Zephyr RTOS includes a wide range of drivers. In this practical exercise, we will take a closer look at the sensor drivers, specifically the LPS22HB sensor driver.</span><div><span style=\"color: rgb(31, 35, 40); font-family: &quot;Mona Sans VF&quot;, -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, &quot;Segoe UI&quot;, &quot;Noto Sans Backtick Fix&quot;, &quot;Noto Sans&quot;, Helvetica, Arial, sans-serif, &quot;Apple Color Emoji&quot;, &quot;Segoe UI Emoji&quot;; font-size: 16px;\"><br></span></div><div><span style=\"color: rgb(31, 35, 40); font-family: &quot;Mona Sans VF&quot;, -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, &quot;Segoe UI&quot;, &quot;Noto Sans Backtick Fix&quot;, &quot;Noto Sans&quot;, Helvetica, Arial, sans-serif, &quot;Apple Color Emoji&quot;, &quot;Segoe UI Emoji&quot;; font-size: 16px;\"><b>Required Hardware and Software</b></span></div><div><ul><li><a href=\"https://www.nordicsemi.com/Products/Development-hardware/nRF54LM20-DK\" rel=\"nofollow\" style=\"background-color: rgba(0, 0, 0, 0); color: rgb(9, 105, 218); text-underline-offset: 0.2rem;\">nRF54LM20DK development kit</a></li><li style=\"margin-top: 0.25em;\">LPS22HB break-out board (e.g. from <a href=\"https://www.adafruit.com/product/4633\" rel=\"nofollow\" style=\"background-color: rgba(0, 0, 0, 0); color: rgb(9, 105, 218); text-underline-offset: 0.2rem;\">Adafruit</a>)</li><li style=\"margin-top: 0.25em;\">QWICC Cable to connect sensor board to dev kit (e.g. from <a href=\"https://www.digikey.de/de/products/detail/sparkfun-electronics/17261/13629019?gclsrc=aw.ds&amp;gad_source=1&amp;gad_campaignid=20179590413&amp;gbraid=0AAAAADrbLlgdC5gLnngSLmZOp0lk_C87F&amp;gclid=EAIaIQobChMIpOuGyrnCkQMVXKb9BR30wzLREAQYCiABEgLT7PD_BwE\" rel=\"nofollow\" style=\"background-color: rgba(0, 0, 0, 0); color: rgb(9, 105, 218); text-underline-offset: 0.2rem;\">Digikey</a>)</li><li style=\"margin-top: 0.25em;\">install the <em>nRF Connect SDK</em> v3.4.0 and <em>Visual Studio Code</em>. The installation process is described <a href=\"https://academy.nordicsemi.com/courses/nrf-connect-sdk-fundamentals/lessons/lesson-1-nrf-connect-sdk-introduction/topic/exercise-1-1/\" rel=\"nofollow\" style=\"background-color: rgba(0, 0, 0, 0); color: rgb(9, 105, 218); text-underline-offset: 0.2rem;\">here</a>.</li></ul></div>",
          "codeBlocks": [],
          "images": [],
          "moreInfo": [],
          "codeBlocksNoCopy": []
        }
      ],
      "description": "<p dir=\"ltr\"><span style=\"color: rgb(31, 35, 40); font-family: &quot;Mona Sans VF&quot;, -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, &quot;Segoe UI&quot;, &quot;Noto Sans Backtick Fix&quot;, &quot;Noto Sans&quot;, Helvetica, Arial, sans-serif, &quot;Apple Color Emoji&quot;, &quot;Segoe UI Emoji&quot;; font-size: 16px;\">The Zephyr RTOS includes a wide range of drivers. In this practical exercise, we will take a closer look at the sensor drivers, specifically the LPS22HB sensor driver.</span></p>"
    },
    {
      "title": "Hands-on Step-by-Step Description",
      "description": "",
      "steps": [
        {
          "title": "Connecting the LPS22HB Breakout Board to the nRF54LM20DK",
          "description": "<span style=\"color: rgb(31, 35, 40); font-family: &quot;Mona Sans VF&quot;, -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, &quot;Segoe UI&quot;, &quot;Noto Sans Backtick Fix&quot;, &quot;Noto Sans&quot;, Helvetica, Arial, sans-serif, &quot;Apple Color Emoji&quot;, &quot;Segoe UI Emoji&quot;; font-size: 16px;\">First connect the LP22HB breakout board to the nRF54LM20DK as shown in the picture:</span>",
          "codeBlocks": [],
          "codeBlocksNoCopy": [],
          "images": [
            {
              "src": "data:image/jpeg;base64,",
              "caption": ""
            }
          ],
          "moreInfo": []
        },
        {
          "title": "Let's start with hello_world Projec",
          "description": "<span style=\"font-size: 14.4px;\">Create a new project based on Zephyr's hello_world example&nbsp; (zephyr/samples/hello_world).</span>",
          "codeBlocks": [],
          "codeBlocksNoCopy": [],
          "images": [],
          "moreInfo": []
        },
        {
          "title": "Include zephyr/kernel.h",
          "description": "<span style=\"font-size: 14.4px;\">The Zephyr hello_world example does not include the zephyr/kernel.h header file. We include this header file in the main.c file:</span>",
          "codeBlocks": [
            {
              "label": "main.c",
              "code": "#include <zephyr/kernel.h>"
            }
          ],
          "codeBlocksNoCopy": [],
          "images": [],
          "moreInfo": []
        },
        {
          "title": "Definition of Hardware Usage in DeviceTree Files",
          "description": "<div><span style=\"font-size: 14.4px;\">Let us now configure the LPS22HB sensor driver in an DeviceTree Overlay file. The Overlay file allows us to overwrite definitions made in the pre-defined board file nrf54lm20dk_nrf54lm20a_cpuapp.dts. We will use the I2C definition of the Arduino Shield and modify it as described in the following text.</span></div><div><span style=\"font-size: 14.4px;\"><br></span></div><div><span style=\"font-size: 14.4px;\">Create the file nrf54lm20dk_nrf54lm20a_cpuapp.overlay in your project folder. <b>IMPORTANT</b>: Make sure you use exactly this name, as we will overwrite the file nrf54lm20dk_nrf54lm20_cpuapp.dts with this name!</span></div><div><span style=\"font-size: 14.4px;\"><br></span></div><div><span style=\"font-size: 14.4px;\">Then add following content to this file:</span></div>",
          "codeBlocks": [
            {
              "label": "nrf54lm20dk_nrf54lm20a_cpuapp.overlay",
              "code": "/* Configuration of a LPS22HB device\n   -------------------------------------------------------\n   Device Address 0x5d is used here. \n   Selected pins:\n        SCL  ->  P1.12\n        SDA  ->  P1.13\n*/    \n&i2c21 {\n       status = \"okay\";\n       pinctrl-0 = <&i2c21_default>;\n       pinctrl-1 = <&i2c21_sleep>;\n       pinctrl-names = \"default\", \"sleep\";\n       clock-frequency = <I2C_BITRATE_STANDARD>;\n\n       lps22hb: lps22hb@5d{\n               compatible = \"st,lps22hb-press\";\n               reg = <0x5d>;\n       };\n};\n\n&pinctrl {\n         i2c21_default: i2c21_default {\n              group1  {\n                  psels = <NRF_PSEL(TWIM_SCL, 1, 12)>,\n                          <NRF_PSEL(TWIM_SDA, 1, 13)>;\n              };\n         };\n\n         i2c21_sleep: i2c21_sleep {\n              group1  {\n                  psels = <NRF_PSEL(TWIM_SCL, 1, 12)>,\n                          <NRF_PSEL(TWIM_SDA, 1, 13)>;\n                  low-power-enable;\n              };\n         };\n};"
            }
          ],
          "codeBlocksNoCopy": [],
          "images": [],
          "moreInfo": []
        },
        {
          "title": "Using the DeviceTree Definitions in our C-Code",
          "description": "<span style=\"color: rgb(31, 35, 40); font-family: &quot;Mona Sans VF&quot;, -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, &quot;Segoe UI&quot;, &quot;Noto Sans Backtick Fix&quot;, &quot;Noto Sans&quot;, Helvetica, Arial, sans-serif, &quot;Apple Color Emoji&quot;, &quot;Segoe UI Emoji&quot;; font-size: 16px;\">In the previous step, we defined the hardware usage for the LPS22HB sensor. Now we need to use these definitions in our C code.</span><div><span style=\"color: rgb(31, 35, 40); font-family: &quot;Mona Sans VF&quot;, -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, &quot;Segoe UI&quot;, &quot;Noto Sans Backtick Fix&quot;, &quot;Noto Sans&quot;, Helvetica, Arial, sans-serif, &quot;Apple Color Emoji&quot;, &quot;Segoe UI Emoji&quot;; font-size: 16px;\"><br></span></div><div><span style=\"color: rgb(31, 35, 40); font-family: &quot;Mona Sans VF&quot;, -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, &quot;Segoe UI&quot;, &quot;Noto Sans Backtick Fix&quot;, &quot;Noto Sans&quot;, Helvetica, Arial, sans-serif, &quot;Apple Color Emoji&quot;, &quot;Segoe UI Emoji&quot;; font-size: 16px;\">Get the device structure from the DeviceTree node with node label \"lps22hb\". This is done by adding following line in main function:</span><span style=\"color: rgb(31, 35, 40); font-family: &quot;Mona Sans VF&quot;, -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, &quot;Segoe UI&quot;, &quot;Noto Sans Backtick Fix&quot;, &quot;Noto Sans&quot;, Helvetica, Arial, sans-serif, &quot;Apple Color Emoji&quot;, &quot;Segoe UI Emoji&quot;; font-size: 16px;\"></span></div>",
          "codeBlocks": [
            {
              "label": "src/main.c - add following lines in void main(void) function",
              "code": "#define SENSOR_NODE DT_NODELABEL(lps22hb)\n    const struct device *const dev = DEVICE_DT_GET(SENSOR_NODE);    \n    if (!device_is_ready(dev)) {\n        printk(\"Sensor is not ready %s\\n\", dev->name);\n        return 0;\n    }\n    printk(\"Sensor is ready!\\n\");"
            }
          ],
          "codeBlocksNoCopy": [],
          "images": [],
          "moreInfo": []
        },
        {
          "title": "KCONFIG: Adding the Sensor Driver and Interface Driver to our Project",
          "description": "<p dir=\"auto\" style=\"margin-top: 16px; margin-bottom: 16px; color: rgb(31, 35, 40); font-family: &quot;Mona Sans VF&quot;, -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, &quot;Segoe UI&quot;, &quot;Noto Sans Backtick Fix&quot;, &quot;Noto Sans&quot;, Helvetica, Arial, sans-serif, &quot;Apple Color Emoji&quot;, &quot;Segoe UI Emoji&quot;; font-size: 16px;\">The required software modules can be added to our project by setting the corresponding CONFIG symbols (KCONFIG). Let's consider which software modules are needed:</p><blockquote style=\"margin: 0px 0px 16px; color: rgb(89, 99, 110); border-left: 3.57143px solid rgb(209, 217, 224); padding: 0px 1em; font-family: &quot;Mona Sans VF&quot;, -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, &quot;Segoe UI&quot;, &quot;Noto Sans Backtick Fix&quot;, &quot;Noto Sans&quot;, Helvetica, Arial, sans-serif, &quot;Apple Color Emoji&quot;, &quot;Segoe UI Emoji&quot;; font-size: 16px;\"><ul dir=\"auto\" style=\"margin-top: 0px; margin-bottom: 0px; padding-left: 2em;\"><li>I2C: The LPS22HB itself supports I2C and SPI interfaces. However, the breakout board is designed so that only I2C is used.</li><li style=\"margin-top: 0.25em;\">Sensor: Include the sensor drivers to get access to the LPS22HB sensor driver.</li></ul></blockquote><p dir=\"auto\" style=\"margin-top: 16px; margin-bottom: 16px; color: rgb(31, 35, 40); font-family: &quot;Mona Sans VF&quot;, -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, &quot;Segoe UI&quot;, &quot;Noto Sans Backtick Fix&quot;, &quot;Noto Sans&quot;, Helvetica, Arial, sans-serif, &quot;Apple Color Emoji&quot;, &quot;Segoe UI Emoji&quot;; font-size: 16px;\">To enable these modules, we need to add following lines to the <span style=\"font-weight: 600;\">prj.conf</span> file.</p>",
          "codeBlocks": [
            {
              "label": "prj.conf",
              "code": "# Enable Software Modules for Sensor Driver Usage\nCONFIG_I2C=y\nCONFIG_SENSOR=y\n\n# Enable Floating Point output \nCONFIG_CBPRINTF_FP_SUPPORT=y"
            }
          ],
          "codeBlocksNoCopy": [],
          "images": [],
          "moreInfo": []
        },
        {
          "title": "Using the LPS22BH Sensor Driver in our own Application",
          "description": "<p dir=\"auto\" style=\"margin-top: 0px; margin-bottom: 16px; color: rgb(31, 35, 40); font-family: &quot;Mona Sans VF&quot;, -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, &quot;Segoe UI&quot;, &quot;Noto Sans Backtick Fix&quot;, &quot;Noto Sans&quot;, Helvetica, Arial, sans-serif, &quot;Apple Color Emoji&quot;, &quot;Segoe UI Emoji&quot;; font-size: 16px;\">We have included the driver with the previous steps. Now we will use the driver in our own Application software.</p><p dir=\"auto\" style=\"margin-top: 0px; margin-bottom: 16px; color: rgb(31, 35, 40); font-family: &quot;Mona Sans VF&quot;, -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, &quot;Segoe UI&quot;, &quot;Noto Sans Backtick Fix&quot;, &quot;Noto Sans&quot;, Helvetica, Arial, sans-serif, &quot;Apple Color Emoji&quot;, &quot;Segoe UI Emoji&quot;; font-size: 16px;\">We keep our software quite simple and handle the sensor measurements in the main entire loop. So insert following lines into the main function:</p>",
          "codeBlocks": [
            {
              "label": "src/main.c - add following lines in void main(void) function",
              "code": "    while(1){\n        struct sensor_value pressure, temp;\n\n        // Fetch a sample from the sensor and store it in an internal driver buffer\n        if (sensor_sample_fetch(dev) < 0) {\n            printk(\"Error: Sensor sample update error\\n\");\n            return;\n        }\n\n        // Get a reading from a sensor device (read pressure)\n        if (sensor_channel_get(dev, SENSOR_CHAN_PRESS, &pressure) < 0) {\n            printk(\"Error: Cannot read LPS22HB pressure channel\\n\");\n            return;\n        }\n\n        // Get a reading from a sensor device (read temperature)\t\n        if (sensor_channel_get(dev, SENSOR_CHAN_AMBIENT_TEMP, &temp) < 0) {\n            printk(\"Error: Cannot read LPS22HB temperature channel\\n\");\n            return;\n        }\n\n        /* display pressure */\n        printk(\"pressure: %d.%d kPa, \", pressure.val1, pressure.val2);\n        //printk(\"Pressure: %.1f kPa, \", sensor_value_to_double(&pressure));\n\n        /* display temperature */\n        printk(\"Temperature: %.1f C\\n\", sensor_value_to_double(&temp));\n\n        k_sleep(K_MSEC(1000));                \n    }"
            }
          ],
          "codeBlocksNoCopy": [],
          "images": [],
          "moreInfo": []
        },
        {
          "title": "Include required Sensing Subsystem Header File",
          "description": "<span style=\"color: rgb(31, 35, 40); font-family: &quot;Mona Sans VF&quot;, -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, &quot;Segoe UI&quot;, &quot;Noto Sans Backtick Fix&quot;, &quot;Noto Sans&quot;, Helvetica, Arial, sans-serif, &quot;Apple Color Emoji&quot;, &quot;Segoe UI Emoji&quot;; font-size: 16px;\">We use the functions </span><span style=\"font-weight: 600; color: rgb(31, 35, 40); font-family: &quot;Mona Sans VF&quot;, -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, &quot;Segoe UI&quot;, &quot;Noto Sans Backtick Fix&quot;, &quot;Noto Sans&quot;, Helvetica, Arial, sans-serif, &quot;Apple Color Emoji&quot;, &quot;Segoe UI Emoji&quot;; font-size: 16px;\">sensor_sample_fetch</span><span style=\"color: rgb(31, 35, 40); font-family: &quot;Mona Sans VF&quot;, -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, &quot;Segoe UI&quot;, &quot;Noto Sans Backtick Fix&quot;, &quot;Noto Sans&quot;, Helvetica, Arial, sans-serif, &quot;Apple Color Emoji&quot;, &quot;Segoe UI Emoji&quot;; font-size: 16px;\"> and </span><span style=\"font-weight: 600; color: rgb(31, 35, 40); font-family: &quot;Mona Sans VF&quot;, -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, &quot;Segoe UI&quot;, &quot;Noto Sans Backtick Fix&quot;, &quot;Noto Sans&quot;, Helvetica, Arial, sans-serif, &quot;Apple Color Emoji&quot;, &quot;Segoe UI Emoji&quot;; font-size: 16px;\">sensor_channel_get</span><span style=\"color: rgb(31, 35, 40); font-family: &quot;Mona Sans VF&quot;, -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, &quot;Segoe UI&quot;, &quot;Noto Sans Backtick Fix&quot;, &quot;Noto Sans&quot;, Helvetica, Arial, sans-serif, &quot;Apple Color Emoji&quot;, &quot;Segoe UI Emoji&quot;; font-size: 16px;\"> to trigger a measurement and read the conversion results. The use of these functions requires their declaration. This is done in the header file </span><span style=\"font-weight: 600; color: rgb(31, 35, 40); font-family: &quot;Mona Sans VF&quot;, -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, &quot;Segoe UI&quot;, &quot;Noto Sans Backtick Fix&quot;, &quot;Noto Sans&quot;, Helvetica, Arial, sans-serif, &quot;Apple Color Emoji&quot;, &quot;Segoe UI Emoji&quot;; font-size: 16px;\">sensor.h</span><span style=\"color: rgb(31, 35, 40); font-family: &quot;Mona Sans VF&quot;, -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, &quot;Segoe UI&quot;, &quot;Noto Sans Backtick Fix&quot;, &quot;Noto Sans&quot;, Helvetica, Arial, sans-serif, &quot;Apple Color Emoji&quot;, &quot;Segoe UI Emoji&quot;; font-size: 16px;\">. We must therefore include them in our project. Insert following line at the beginning of the main.c file:</span>",
          "codeBlocks": [
            {
              "label": "src/main.c",
              "code": "#include <zephyr/drivers/sensor.h>"
            }
          ],
          "codeBlocksNoCopy": [],
          "images": [],
          "moreInfo": []
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Testing",
      "description": "",
      "steps": [
        {
          "title": "Supply voltage on nRF54LM20DK",
          "description": "<span style=\"color: rgb(89, 99, 110); font-family: &quot;Mona Sans VF&quot;, -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, &quot;Segoe UI&quot;, &quot;Noto Sans Backtick Fix&quot;, &quot;Noto Sans&quot;, Helvetica, Arial, sans-serif, &quot;Apple Color Emoji&quot;, &quot;Segoe UI Emoji&quot;; font-size: 16px;\">Usually the default supply voltage of the development kit is 1.8 V. This is not enough for the sensor board. So we have to adjust the nRF54L dev kit supply voltage by opening </span><a href=\"https://docs.nordicsemi.com/r/bundle/nrf-connect-for-desktop/page/board-configurator-app\" rel=\"nofollow\" style=\"color: rgb(9, 105, 218); text-underline-offset: 0.2rem; font-family: &quot;Mona Sans VF&quot;, -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, &quot;Segoe UI&quot;, &quot;Noto Sans Backtick Fix&quot;, &quot;Noto Sans&quot;, Helvetica, Arial, sans-serif, &quot;Apple Color Emoji&quot;, &quot;Segoe UI Emoji&quot;; font-size: 16px;\"><em>Board Configurator</em></a><span style=\"color: rgb(89, 99, 110); font-family: &quot;Mona Sans VF&quot;, -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, &quot;Segoe UI&quot;, &quot;Noto Sans Backtick Fix&quot;, &quot;Noto Sans&quot;, Helvetica, Arial, sans-serif, &quot;Apple Color Emoji&quot;, &quot;Segoe UI Emoji&quot;; font-size: 16px;\"> and increasing the supply voltage to 3.0 V.</span>",
          "codeBlocks": [],
          "codeBlocksNoCopy": [],
          "images": [],
          "moreInfo": []
        },
        {
          "title": "Build the project and download the project",
          "description": "Please perform a pristine build and afterwards flash it.",
          "codeBlocks": [],
